> This is not a standard definition, and it agrees neither with what the
> inventor of the WWW says nor with Google's most popular definition.
> 
> The world wide web is a network of hypertext (HTML) documents.  Things
> in the web can link to things outside the web.  Not everything
> delivered over HTTP or HTTPS is part of the web--text files and other
> non-hypertext are often served over HTTP for the sake of convenience.
> Usually the graphical images, videos, etc that are actually embedded
> inside a web page are also consider part of the web themselves, though
> some strict definitions omit them.
> 
> As Tim Berners Lee, the inventor of the World Wide Web, puts it in his
> FAQ:
> " Lots of different sort of programs use the Internet: electronic
> mail, for example, was around long before the global hypertext system
> I invented and called the World Wide Web ('Web). Now,
> videoconferencing and streamed audio channels are among other things
> which, like the Web, encode information in different ways and use
> different languages between computers ("protocols") to provide a
> service." 
> 
> Note that things like streamed audio channels--which you can click on
> from Web pages to download, and are often served over http--are
> outside of his definition, which is limited to the hypertext system
> itself and not everything it links to or everything that can be viewed
> in a browser.  He's made similar statements in other speeches.
> 
> The first definition from Google "define:www" is:
> "a system of Internet servers that support specially formatted
> documents. The documents are formatted in a language called HTML
> (HyperText Markup Language) that supports links to other documents, as
> well as graphics, audio, and video files. This means you can jump from
> one document to another simply by clicking on hot spots. Not all
> Internet servers are part of the World Wide Web."
> 
> Which also says that the network of hypertext documents (including
> their embedded graphics, video, etc) comprises the Web.
